<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaritus of Brindisi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1197</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaritus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a> was an admiral of the Norman Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Commanding the fleet of William II, he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and other Ionian islands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1185 and became their first count palatine. After the fall of the Hauteville dynasty he was arrested by Emperor Henry VI in 1194 and died in captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaritus-of-brindisi.jpg" alt="Margaritus of Brindisi" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FocalPoint for this adaptation Original map : de:Captain BloodTranslation : Eric Gaba (Sting) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Normans_possessions_12century-fr_Margaritus_of_Brindisi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margaritus, called the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a>, was the last great ammiratus ammiratorum, or grand admiral, of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, following Christodulus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-antioch">George of Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maio-of-bari">Maio of Bari</a> in that office. He probably began as a Greek pirate and rose to be a privateer before becoming a permanent admiral of the navy. He married Marina or Martina, an illegitimate daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a>. He first appears in the record beside Tancred, then Count of Lecce, as a leader of the fleet that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a> and then captured many ships of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 2 October 1187, King William II sent Margaritus to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. With 60 ships and 200 knights he patrolled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> coast and kept Saladin from taking the vital seaports of the crusader kingdom; in July 1188 he arrived at Tripoli and forced Saladin to raise the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers">Krak des Chevaliers</a>, and similar episodes followed at Marqab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. His fleet returned after William died on 11 November 1189, and on 4 October 1190 he fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> when Richard the Lionheart sacked and burnt the city.</p><p>A staunch supporter of Tancred against Henry VI, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, who claimed the kingdom through his wife Constance, Margaritus defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> when Henry besieged it in 1191, harassing the Pisan navy and keeping the harbour open. When the people of Salerno captured Constance, he carried her to Tancred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, and Tancred made him the first Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1192.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A second, larger fleet under Markward von Anweiler reached the bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 25 August 1194, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, where Margaritus held the citadel, surrendered on 20 November. According to Roger of Howden, Henry confirmed him as admiral and gave him the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>, probably meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>. Four days after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> coronation he was arrested with Nicholas, Archbishop of Salerno, Tancred's widow Sibylla of Acerra and his brief successor William III on charges of conspiracy. Blinded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, he died there in 1197. Karl Hopf's undocumented conjecture that his daughters married the later rulers of Cephalonia and Corfu has been rejected by modern scholarship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margaritus of Brindisi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1185 the Norman admiral <b>Margaritus of Brindisi</b> took Cephalonia and Zakynthos from Byzantium for William II of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-cephalonia-and-zakynthos">County Palatine of Cephalonia and Zakynthos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in the Kingdom of Germany. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.64,17.95&amp;year=1187" aria-label="Brindisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4990 712 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Margaritus of Brindisi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5440 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5440 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.64,17.95&amp;year=1187">What was here in 1187? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
